WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.550
This lesson is where the momentum story becomes a safety story.

2
00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:09.100
A moving car carries momentum and kinetic energy.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.650
To stop it, the car's momentum must be changed to zero.

4
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.200
The average braking force depends on how large that momentum change is and how quickly it happens.

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.750
So this is not only a formula lesson.

6
00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:27.300
It is a lesson about why speed and stopping time matter so much in real roads and real safety decisions.

7
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.850
Keep this takeaway in view: Braking force is a rate-of-change story, not just a stopping story.

8
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:36.400
Momentum means what must change.

9
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.950
The car has to lose its forward momentum to stop.

10
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.500
Time means how fast it changes.

11
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.050
A shorter stopping time means a larger average force.

12
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:55.791
This visual is powerful because it keeps three ideas visible together.

13
00:00:55.791 --> 00:01:01.532
Momentum grows with speed.

14
00:01:01.532 --> 00:01:07.273
Kinetic energy also grows with speed, and it grows strongly.

15
00:01:07.273 --> 00:01:13.014
Then the stopping force depends on how quickly the momentum is brought down to zero.

16
00:01:13.014 --> 00:01:18.756
The learner should see that braking is not only about pressing harder.

17
00:01:18.756 --> 00:01:24.497
It is about the initial motion state and the time allowed for the stop.

18
00:01:24.497 --> 00:01:30.238
Keep this takeaway in view: Keep speed and stopping time in the same mental picture when judging braking force.

19
00:01:30.238 --> 00:01:35.979
Higher speed: Greater speed means greater momentum to remove.

20
00:01:35.979 --> 00:01:41.720
Stopping time: More time lowers the average force for the same momentum change.

21
00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:46.756
Imagine catching a ball.

22
00:01:46.756 --> 00:01:51.792
If your hands stay stiff and stop it almost instantly, the force feels large.

23
00:01:51.792 --> 00:01:56.828
If your hands move back and take a little longer to stop the ball, the force feels smaller, even though the ball still has to be brought to rest.

24
00:01:56.828 --> 00:02:01.864
That is the same logic as braking.

25
00:02:01.864 --> 00:02:06.900
The total momentum change still has to happen, but a longer time softens the average force.

26
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.936
Keep this takeaway in view: Longer stopping time is one of the simplest ways to reduce stopping force.

27
00:02:11.936 --> 00:02:16.972
Stiff catch means short time.

28
00:02:16.972 --> 00:02:22.008
A short stopping interval produces a larger average force.

29
00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:27.044
Soft catch means longer time.

30
00:02:27.044 --> 00:02:32.080
Extra time lowers the force needed to stop the ball.

31
00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:38.166
The main relation here is average force equals change in momentum divided by time.

32
00:02:38.166 --> 00:02:44.252
That is just the braking version of the impulse bridge from the previous lesson.

33
00:02:44.252 --> 00:02:50.338
It tells you the force depends on two things: how much momentum has to be removed, and how quickly that removal happens.

34
00:02:50.338 --> 00:02:56.424
The lesson also keeps kinetic energy visible because higher speed makes the overall stop more demanding in more than one way.

35
00:02:56.424 --> 00:03:02.510
Keep this takeaway in view: Calculate the force through momentum change, then explain the safety meaning through speed and time.

36
00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:08.596
Momentum: Find the starting momentum from mass and velocity.

37
00:03:08.596 --> 00:03:14.682
Force: Divide the momentum change by the stopping time.

38
00:03:14.682 --> 00:03:20.768
F = Delta p / Delta t.

39
00:03:20.768 --> 00:03:26.854
p = m v.

40
00:03:26.854 --> 00:03:32.940
Use momentum to find the stopping force, and keep kinetic energy in mind for the safety interpretation.

41
00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:38.035
If the mass stays the same and speed doubles, momentum doubles.

42
00:03:38.035 --> 00:03:43.131
That alone makes the stopping force larger if the stopping time is unchanged.

43
00:03:43.131 --> 00:03:48.226
Kinetic energy rises even more strongly because speed is squared.

44
00:03:48.226 --> 00:03:53.322
So higher speed is demanding in two linked ways.

45
00:03:53.322 --> 00:03:58.417
There is more motion to remove, and the energy involved is much larger.

46
00:03:58.417 --> 00:04:03.513
That is why high-speed braking is such a big safety issue.

47
00:04:03.513 --> 00:04:08.608
Keep this takeaway in view: Speed multiplies the seriousness of braking, not just the number in the question.

48
00:04:08.608 --> 00:04:13.704
Double speed means double momentum.

49
00:04:13.704 --> 00:04:18.799
The car carries twice as much momentum at the same mass.

50
00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:23.895
Double speed means four times kinetic energy.

51
00:04:23.895 --> 00:04:28.990
The energy demand rises much more strongly.

52
00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:33.105
Try three checks.

53
00:04:33.105 --> 00:04:37.219
What relation gives the average braking force?

54
00:04:37.219 --> 00:04:41.334
Change in momentum divided by time.

55
00:04:41.334 --> 00:04:45.448
If the same car stops over a longer time, what happens to the average force?

56
00:04:45.448 --> 00:04:49.563
It decreases.

57
00:04:49.563 --> 00:04:53.678
And why does higher speed make braking harder?

58
00:04:53.678 --> 00:04:57.792
Because the car begins with larger momentum and much larger kinetic energy.

59
00:04:57.792 --> 00:05:01.907
Those checks bring the whole safety picture together.

60
00:05:01.907 --> 00:05:06.022
Keep this takeaway in view: The lesson is stable once speed, momentum, and stopping time are all linked in one picture.

61
00:05:06.022 --> 00:05:10.136
Force relation means Delta p over Delta t.

62
00:05:10.136 --> 00:05:14.251
Average force comes from how quickly the momentum changes.

63
00:05:14.251 --> 00:05:18.365
Longer stop means smaller force.

64
00:05:18.365 --> 00:05:22.480
The same change spread over more time is gentler.

65
00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:27.271
You are ready for the worked example now.

66
00:05:27.271 --> 00:05:32.062
A 1000 kilogram car moving at 20 metres per second has momentum 1000 times 20, which is 20000 kilogram metres per second.

67
00:05:32.062 --> 00:05:36.852
Since the car stops, that whole momentum is removed.

68
00:05:36.852 --> 00:05:41.643
If the stopping time is 0.5 seconds, divide 20000 by 0.5 to get an average braking force of 40000 newtons.

69
00:05:41.643 --> 00:05:46.434
If the same stop takes 1.0 second, divide by the longer time instead and get 20000 newtons.

70
00:05:46.434 --> 00:05:51.225
The explanation matters: the same momentum change spread over more time gives a smaller average force, which is why longer stopping time improves safety.

71
00:05:51.225 --> 00:05:56.016
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: calculate the force from momentum change, then explain the safety benefit of longer time.

72
00:05:56.016 --> 00:06:00.807
Step 1: Calculate the car's starting momentum from mass and speed.

73
00:06:00.807 --> 00:06:05.598
Step 2: Use the stopping time to find the average braking force.

74
00:06:05.598 --> 00:06:10.388
p = 1000 x 20 = 20000 kg m/s.

75
00:06:10.388 --> 00:06:15.179
F = 20000 / 0.5 = 40000 N.

76
00:06:15.179 --> 00:06:19.970
State clearly that the force falls because the same momentum change is spread over longer time.
